WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Why does a hawk need grass?

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.060
In about thirty minutes, picture cards and yarn let your child see the answer.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.080
They practice looking closely, sorting living things, and testing a guess by hand.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.853
Gather twelve to eighteen index cards or paper squares, crayons or markers, and a

5
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:27.773
ball of yarn. You also need tape or paper clips, a clear floor or table, and

6
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:29.053
scissors just for you.

7
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.513
Review the yarn safety rules before you start.

8
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:37.707
Draw cards for sun, grass, grasshopper, frog, snake, and hawk.

9
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.327
Line them up in that order.

10
00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:44.547
Ask, "Where does the grass go when the grasshopper eats it?"

11
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:46.687
Point the arrow toward the grasshopper.

12
00:00:47.827 --> 00:00:50.347
The first arrow shows sunlight reaching the grass.

13
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.380
Grass makes food using sunlight.

14
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.800
Animals are consumers.

15
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.020
Mushrooms break down dead material.

16
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.340
For the Bay chain, draw sun, phytoplankton, zooplankton, and menhaden, in that

17
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.000
order. From menhaden, point an arrow to striped bass and another to osprey.

18
00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:15.723
Add rabbit, mouse, fox, mushroom, and earthworm cards.

19
00:01:17.023 --> 00:01:19.003
Each person holds one to three cards.

20
00:01:20.063 --> 00:01:22.783
Start at grass and pass yarn from food to eater.

21
00:01:24.103 --> 00:01:26.783
Link living cards to mushroom and earthworm too.

22
00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:30.143
The overlapping chains make a food web.

23
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.720
Have the snake holder give one gentle tug.

24
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.200
Ask anyone who feels it to raise a hand.

25
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.720
Now look at the grass card.

26
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.460
Ask, "If we take out the grass, who loses a food link first?"

27
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:51.503
Take out the grass card.

28
00:01:52.903 --> 00:01:55.563
Trace the links to grasshopper, rabbit, and mouse first.

29
00:01:56.903 --> 00:01:58.943
Then follow their links to other animals.

30
00:02:00.223 --> 00:02:01.823
The yarn shows which cards connect.

31
00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.643
Place a tadpole near producers.

32
00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:08.283
Most tadpoles eat algae and plant bits.

33
00:02:09.643 --> 00:02:10.863
Adult frogs eat insects.

34
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:15.073
Stay with your child the whole time and do all the cutting.

35
00:02:16.593 --> 00:02:17.593
Hold yarn loosely.

36
00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:19.713
Keep it off necks.

37
00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.373
Never wrap it tightly around fingers or wrists.

38
00:02:24.833 --> 00:02:25.833
Hand the ball over.

39
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:28.413
Stay still while holding yarn.

40
00:02:29.653 --> 00:02:32.233
Clear yarn and loose cards before anyone moves.

41
00:02:33.473 --> 00:02:37.033
If you work outside, wash hands with soap and water afterward.

42
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:44.007
Yarn, string, tape, paper clips, marker caps, and small crayon pieces can be choking

43
00:02:44.007 --> 00:02:47.827
hazards. Keep them away from children under four.

44
00:02:49.107 --> 00:02:50.547
Call nine one one if choking.

45
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.780
Through this game, your child practices asking what happens next and checking a

46
00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.060
guess against real links.

47
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.580
By the end, one gentle tug shows why a hawk still needs sunlight and grass.
